AIDS is an infectious disease caused by HIV which is recognised as having no geographical, social, political or economic boundaries and for which no cure has yet been found; the spread of this disease has been and is seriously affecting security, stability and socio-economic development, and requires the State to adopt multi-sectoral response measures in order to:
1. Promote public awareness among all citizens of the causes, the modes of transmission, the consequences of HIV/AIDS, the means of preventing it and the fight against its spread, through education programmes and nationwide information campaigns;
2. Prohibit all forms of discrimination against any person who is suspected or known to be infected with HIV or living with AIDS;
3. Promote universal precautions in all methods and practices that may give rise to a risk of HIV/AIDS transmission;
4. Take appropriate measures in respect of all factors that increase vulnerability to the spread of HIV/AIDS;
5. Encourage the potential role of persons living with HIV/AIDS in disclosing information and their own experience in order to raise public awareness;
